Yes, you can absolutely build and run a WPF (Windows Presentation Foundation) project on Xubuntu using Wine. However, because WPF relies heavily on Windows-specific APIs (like DirectX and milcore.dll), there are major caveats and better native alternatives to consider depending on your goal.
The Alternatives: Are there better options?

Before diving into Wine, you should know that native Linux development is almost always preferred over Wine emulation for .NET UI apps.

    Avalonia UI (Best Alternative / Open Source): If you are open to making your app truly cross-platform, Avalonia is a modern, powerful UI framework. Its XAML syntax is incredibly close to WPF, and it runs natively on Linux, macOS, and Windows.

    Avalonia XPF (Commercial): This is a drop-in replacement binary that allows you to run unmodified WPF apps natively on Linux/macOS. It swaps out the Windows DirectX backend with a cross-platform rendering engine (Skia). However, it requires a paid commercial license.

If your absolute goal is to keep the project strictly as a standard WPF app and use Wine to build/run it on Xubuntu, follow the guide below.
Step-by-Step Guide: Building & Running WPF via Wine

To do this, we cannot use the native Linux .NET SDK. Instead, we must install the Windows version of the .NET SDK inside your Wine prefix so it has access to the Windows-only WPF compiler targets.
Step 1: Install Wine on Xubuntu

Open your terminal and install Wine along with winetricks (which helps install necessary Windows fonts and components).
Bash

sudo dpkg --add-architecture i386 
sudo apt update
sudo apt install wine64 wine32 winetricks -y

Step 2: Configure your Wine Prefix

Create a clean, isolated 64-bit Wine environment (prefix) for your .NET development.
Bash

export WINEPREFIX=~/.wine_dotnet
export WINEARCH=win64
winecfg

Note: A window will pop up. If Wine asks you to install "Mono" or "Gecko", click Cancel. You do not want Wine Mono, as it will conflict with the official Microsoft .NET SDK.
Step 3: Install Core Components & Fonts

WPF apps often crash on Linux if standard Windows fonts or rendering properties are missing. Use winetricks to fix this:
Bash

winetricks corefonts

Step 4: Install the Windows .NET SDK inside Wine

    Go to the official Microsoft .NET download website using your Linux browser.

    Download the Windows x64 Installer (.exe) for the version of .NET your project uses (e.g., .NET 8.0 or .NET 9.0).

    In your terminal, run the installer using Wine:

Bash

wine ~/Downloads/dotnet-sdk-*-win-x64.exe

Follow the standard Windows installation wizard prompts to complete the setup.
Step 5: Build your WPF Project

Navigate to your WPF project directory (where your .csproj file lives) and use Wine to call the Windows dotnet.exe compiler.
Bash

# Verify Wine can see the .NET SDK
wine "C:\Program Files\dotnet\dotnet.exe" --version

# Build the project
wine "C:\Program Files\dotnet\dotnet.exe" build -c Release

Step 6: Run your WPF App

Once the compilation succeeds, navigate to your output folder (usually bin/Release/net8.0-windows/) and execute your application:
Bash

cd bin/Release/net*-windows/
wine YourAppName.exe

⚠️ Important Troubleshooting & Limitations

    Performance: Because Wine has to translate DirectX/Direct3D calls into OpenGL or Vulkan, you might notice UI lag or visual glitches in animations.

    Debugging: Debugging a WPF app running under Wine from a Linux IDE (like VS Code or JetBrains Rider) is incredibly difficult to set up. If you plan to actively develop the app on Xubuntu, migrating the UI code to Avalonia UI will save you massive headaches.
